// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: generics, soundness, parametricity
// @timeout: 30000
// @expected-error: E400
// @expected-error: E400
// @expected-error: E400
// @expected-error-count: 3
// @description: A function's type parameters are RIGID inside its own body.
//
//   `fn f<T>(t: T) -> T` promises to work for every T the CALLER picks, so
//   the body may not decide what T is. All three functions below break that
//   promise in a different direction and each must be rejected at the line
//   that breaks it — not at a call site, and not as an ambiguity somewhere
//   else. There is no call to any of them in this file: that is the point,
//   because the defect this pins was invisible without one.
//
//   Measured before the fix: all three checked CLEAN. T was an ordinary
//   inference variable shared by the body and every caller, so the body
//   simply solved it — to Int, to Text, to whatever the mistake implied.
//   Adding a call site DID produce an error, at the caller, about code the
//   caller had not written.
//
//   The root was that a type parameter existed as TWO variables.
//   `check_function_inner` walks `func.generics` twice: once before the
//   parameter and return types are built, and again after entering the
//   body's scope, where it minted a second fresh variable and redefined the
//   name with it. So `T` in the SIGNATURE and `T` in the BODY were different
//   variables, and two different variables cannot contradict each other.
//   That is why the third function below — a concrete declared return fed
//   from a parameter — survived even the first attempt at making the
//   signature's T rigid: the body was not using that T at all.
//
//   The positive pole is `type_parameters_stay_usable.vr`, and it is not
//   optional: a rule this sharp is one bad predicate away from rejecting
//   every generic function in the standard library.
//
//   Task: T0793.

// The body yields Int where the caller was promised T.
fn returns_wrong_type<T>(t: T) -> T {
    42
}

// Same shape, different type, to show the diagnostic follows the value and
// is not a special case for integers.
fn returns_wrong_type_text<T>(t: T) -> T {
    "oops"
}

// The mirror image: a CONCRETE declared return fed from a parameter. This is
// the direction that survived the first fix, and it is the one that the
// two-variables root actually explains.
fn concrete_return_from_parameter<T>(t: T) -> Int {
    t
}

fn main() {
    print("type parameters are rigid");
}
